''[[vocab/demo/HELLO]]''

<<.tid-source "vocab/demo/HELLO">>

''[[vocab/demo/GREETING]]''

<<.tid-source "vocab/demo/GREETING">>

At parse time each marker emits:

```
<$transclude $variable=".greet" $mode="block" name="..." mood="..." body="..."/>
```

The `attributes` JSON IS the procedure call. `$variable` picks the procedure; the other JSON keys become procedure parameters; `src-name` says which parameter receives the marker body; `$mode="block"` forces block-mode parsing so each call renders as its own paragraph.

!! The procedure

[[.greet-procedure]] (tagged `$:/tags/Macro` so it loads globally):

><<.tid-source ".greet-procedure">>

Note the procedure body is pure inline content. No `<p>` wrapper. The `<p>` comes from `$transclude $mode="block"` re-parsing the body in block mode at render time, where TW's paragraph rule wraps inline runs in `<p>`.

!! Why `$mode="block"` matters

`$transclude $variable=X` re-parses X's source text at render time, in the mode given by `$mode`. Block mode hits TW's paragraph rule, wrapping inline runs in `<p>`. Without `$mode="block"` the procedure body would render as inline content and two consecutive markers would stack side-by-side.

''Pitfall:'' do NOT also wrap the procedure body in `<p>`. Combined with `$mode="block"` you get nested `<p><p>...</p></p>` which browsers normalise by closing the outer `<p>` early. Pick one: either the procedure ships its own block wrapper (and the marker omits `$mode`), or the procedure stays inline (and the marker uses `$mode="block"`). The second path is recommended.

!! Legacy alternative: `$macrocall`

The older `<$macrocall $name=".greet" .../>` widget still works, but it renders the procedure's pre-parsed tree verbatim with no re-parse, so `$mode` does not apply. To get block output via `$macrocall`, the procedure body itself must contain a block-level element. The modern `$transclude` form is preferred for new markers.

!! Limitation

Only ONE parameter can be dynamic per marker (the body, named by `src-name`). All other parameters are static, set in the marker's `attributes` field. To pass a different dynamic value per invocation you currently need the legacy `\custom ... _params="..."` syntax (see [[Example - _params with spaces]]).

